WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
In a perfectly optimized system, execution looks like this.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:10.000
Continuous uninterrupted pulses of data, bypassing traditional hardware bottlenecks to run instantly.

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.000
By replacing the default JavaScript engine in React Native,

4
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:20.000
engineers have eliminated up to 800 milliseconds from application cold start times on real devices.

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.000
The historical culprit for slow mobile startups is the just in time or JIT compilation model,

6
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.000
a standard utilized by older engines like JavaScript Core.

7
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.000
JIT compilation was engineered for long running browser sessions.

8
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:38.000
Here, sustaining peak performance over hours of continuous use is prioritized over the initial loading speed.

9
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.000
Mobile environments operate under severe constraints.

10
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Applications face strict battery limitations, limited memory availability,

11
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.000
and an absolute requirement for immediate UI responsiveness the second and app is opened.

12
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.000
This split-screen flow demonstrates the shift required to solve this constraint.

13
00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:00.000
On the left, the JIT model forces the smartphone to grind through compilation.

14
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.000
On the right, compilation shifts entirely to a server rack.

15
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.000
This is ahead of time, or AOT compilation.

16
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.000
The new architecture operates in two highly specialized layers.

17
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.000
The Hermes engine accelerates the internal JavaScript execution,

18
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:23.000
while the JavaScript interface, or JSI, accelerates external communication with native C++ layers.

19
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Achieving immediate mobile responsiveness requires abandoning web-centric engine architectures

20
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.000
in favor of mobile native memory management and synchronous execution.

21
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:38.000
This side-by-side execution timeline maps CPU and memory utilization.

22
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:43.000
On the top track, a standard JIT engine executes mechanical faces directly on the device.

23
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Raw code moves through the parser, builds a massive abstract syntax tree,

24
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.000
and forces a heavy JIT warm-up phase before rendering.

25
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.000
Contrast this with the Hermes pipeline on the bottom track.

26
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.000
The Hermes CLI performs these heavy tasks during the build step,

27
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:04.000
moving the parser and AST faces off the device entirely and into a separate build server.

28
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.000
The output of this build step is Hermes bytecode, or .HBC.

29
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:13.000
This is a dense, register-based instruction set bundled directly into the application.

30
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:18.000
Because the Hermes bytecode is pre-confiled, the mobile device skips the parser,

31
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.000
AST generation, and JIT warm-up entirely.

32
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.000
Execution starts immediately.

33
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.000
A secondary benefit is drastically reduced RAM usage.

34
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:32.000
Because compilation happens before deployment, no JIT compiler remains resident in the device's memory.

35
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.000
The metrics from this pipeline are clear.

36
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:39.000
Faster cold start times, a lower memory footprint, and a smaller compiled binary.

37
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.000
By offloading computational lifting to the build machine,

38
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.000
Hermes guarantees the mobile CTU is exclusively dedicated to rendering the UI upon launch.

39
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:52.000
An isolated fast engine needs efficient communication with native operating systems.

40
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Internal speed must translate to host communication.

41
00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.000
This structural flow chart demonstrates the connection.

42
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:02.000
The Hermes engine is wrapped by the Hermes runtime,

43
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.000
which communicates directly with the JavaScript interface,

44
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:09.000
establishing a solid bridge to the C++ host layer.

45
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:14.000
In the Legacy React native bridge, every layout request was serialized into JSON

46
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:19.000
and placed in a delayed asynchronous queue before the native side could read it.

47
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:25.000
JSI bypasses that queue by allowing the C++ layer to expose its functions directly

48
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:30.000
into the JavaScript runtime memory space, enabling immediate synchronous function calls.

49
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.000
The React native fabric renderer relies on this capability specifically for its measurement and layout queries.

50
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.000
Layout measurements are returned synchronously within the same UI frame.

51
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:46.000
Engineers no longer need complex asynchronous callback loops to determine where an element belongs.

52
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:52.000
JSI transitions React native from a system that requests native actions via a queue

53
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:56.000
into a system that directly commands native actions in real time.

54
00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:02.000
Moving heavy binary payloads like image pixels or raw audio samples between environments

55
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:06.000
has historically caused massive latency due to data serialization.

56
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:12.000
By locking the layers together, JSI allows heavy data streams to flow between environments instantly

57
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.000
without physical duplication or copying.

58
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:18.000
This diagram shows how JSI utilizes a shared array buffer.

59
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.000
By placing a unified memory block between the layers,

60
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:25.000
pointers from both sides can reference the exact same memory address.

61
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:29.000
This zero copy transfer eliminates the memory duplication and serialization overhead

62
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:32.000
that previously hindered media heavy applications.

63
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.000
However, accessing the shared memory comes with a strict constraint.

64
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.000
The JSI runtime is not thread safe.

65
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.000
All JSI operations must happen exclusively on the JavaScript thread.

66
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.000
Blocking IO cannot be called synchronously without triggering a fading runtime collision.

67
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:51.000
When work is IO bound or requires a different thread,

68
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:55.000
engineers explicitly schedule it asynchronously using standard promises,

69
00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:59.000
routing it back onto the JSI thread through a native invoker hook.

70
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:03.000
Synchronous execution requires precise architectural discipline.

71
00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:07.000
You must explicitly manage thread boundaries to utilize memory sharing safely.

72
00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:13.000
Validating these architectural optimizations in a production environment requires specific empirical measurement tools.

73
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:18.000
Modern React Native architectures bypass older debugging tools like flipper.

74
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:22.000
Instead, Hermes integrates directly with the Chrome Dev Tools Protocol or CDP.

75
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:27.000
This integration allows engineers to inspect the engine directly via a Chrome browser window.

76
00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:32.000
You can map execution paths and set exact breakpoints inside your C++ host functions.

77
00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:35.000
This is a Perfetto system trace.

78
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:42.000
It is the primary tool for profiling JSI overhead and proving synchronous execution claims on Android hardware,

79
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:46.000
displaying host function calls as precise horizontal slices.

80
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:50.000
By analyzing this trace, engineers can spot JSI bottlenecks.

81
00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:55.000
If a horizontal execution slice on the JSI thread is unexpectedly long,

82
00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:00.000
it indicates a host function is doing too much work synchronously and needs to be offloaded.

83
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.000
When failures do occur, JSI exceptions are surface natively.

84
00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:08.000
They appear in the JSI error boundary as standard error objects,

85
00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:12.000
with exact stack traces pointing directly to the failing C++ function.

86
00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.000
The new architecture relies completely on this dual engine integration.

87
00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:19.000
Hermes handles the instant ahead of time by code execution,

88
00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:23.000
while JSI manages direct synchronous host communication.

89
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:27.000
Optimizing modern react native requires moving past the JavaScript layer.

90
00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:34.000
High performance application design now demands a strict understanding of the underlying C++ execution pipelines.

